(Study/ledger: a human creates an exploratory or confirmatory study and registers every candidate variant) t
(before execution. Variants remain visible as registered, completed, failed, excluded, or abandoned; the) t
(last two require a reason. Closing requires all registered variants to be accounted for.) t
(Evidence: sources include quoted passage, content digest, locator, assessment status, and correction lineage.) t
(Findings are candidates until an eligible human accepts supported citations and appropriate study state.) t
(Review: finding reaffirmation, narrowing, supersession, and terminal retraction append history. Corrections) t
(preserve source versions and mark dependent findings for review rather than silently rewriting history.) t
(Goal-to-decision: immutable experiment design revisions carry goal/success criterion, baseline/intervention,) t
(attempt provenance, actual custody links, human-reported observation distinction, and reviewed conclusion) t
(lineage. Operational succeeded and human conclusion cards remain labelled scientific_validity=not_assessed.) t
rule
(Artifact and claim separation) h
(An execution attempt may link an operation and/or explicitly human-reported observation. A provider receipt) t
(does not validate an artifact; a validated artifact does not accept a result; a result-bookkeeping entry) t
(does not create a scientific finding. Export is a bounded complete ledger projection, not a reproducible) t
(bundle of code, environments, released bytes, reports, or universally valid claims.) t
rule
(Labbook integrity properties) h
(The labbook is a permission-filtered projection of control transitions and versioned evidence, not a second) t
(authority. It includes record IDs, actor/time/revision/provenance/access labels. Historical views are) t
(read-only. Design calls for explicit disclosure of incomplete/restricted history and projection lag.) t
(Synthetic integrated coverage deliberately retains all 80 variants rather than selecting favorable results.) t
